2c:["$","div","3",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"If you report a concern and it is not acted on, and the patient still worries you, escalate again. Say clearly that you are concerned and why, and ask for the patient to be reviewed. If you get nowhere, go to the charge nurse. Most facilities also have a rapid response team, and in many, any staff member may activate it. Being wrong about a deteriorating patient costs a few minutes of someone's time; being right and staying quiet costs far more."]}]
2d:["$","figure","4",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200","children":["$","table",null,{"className":"w-full text-left border-collapse","children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50","children":[["$","th","0",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Event"}],["$","th","1",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Do"}],["$","th","2",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Do not"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Fall"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Stay with them, call for help, keep them still until assessed"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Do not lift them up before a nurse assesses them"}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Seizure"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Protect the head, clear the area, time it, turn them on their side afterwards"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Do not restrain them or put anything in their mouth"}]]}],["$","tr","2",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Choking, able to cough"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Encourage coughing; stay with them"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Do not slap the back or intervene while coughing is effective"}]]}],["$","tr","3",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Choking, cannot speak or cough"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Call for help. If trained, alternate 5 back blows with 5 abdominal thrusts (chest thrusts for a late-pregnancy patient or one you cannot reach around) until the object comes out or the patient becomes unresponsive, then start CPR. This is the 2025 American Heart Association sequence"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Do not leave them"}]]}],["$","tr","4",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Unresponsive"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Call a code, check breathing and pulse, begin CPR if trained"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Do not leave to find someone"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"Immediate responses within your scope"}]]}]
2e:["$","div","5",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-blue-50 border-blue-400","children":[false,"After a fall, never lift the patient until a nurse has assessed them. A hip fracture or head injury can be made significantly worse by moving someone. Keep them still and comfortable, call for help, and stay with them."]}]
2f:["$","$L31","6",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"You report a concern about a patient's new confusion. The nurse is busy and says they will look later. The patient still worries you. What should you do?","options":["Wait, since the nurse has assessed the priority","Escalate again, stating clearly that you are concerned and asking for the patient to be reviewed, and go to the charge nurse if needed","Document your concern and move on","Monitor and report at handoff"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"New confusion is among the earliest signs of serious deterioration, including sepsis. If your concern is not acted on and the patient still worries you, escalating again is appropriate and expected. Most facilities also allow any staff member to activate a rapid response team.","optionRationales":["The nurse prioritized without seeing what you saw. Your persistent concern is new information.","Correct. Escalate clearly, and go up the chain if the concern is not addressed.","Documenting without escalating records the concern but does not get the patient seen.","Handoff may be hours away, and new confusion can deteriorate quickly."]}}]
